  "knowledge": "PostgreSQL answers COMMIT with the command tag COMMIT, so the client knows that the transaction committed.",
  "api_outcome": {
    "reported": "committed",
    "basis": "Transaction::commit returned Ok",
    "contract": "Transaction's documentation says that if neither commit nor rollback is called before the value goes out of scope, rollback is called on drop. Drop (sqlx-core/src/transaction.rs) calls PgTransactionManager::start_rollback. That method (sqlx-postgres/src/transaction.rs) queues a simple-query ROLLBACK or ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT and decrements the transaction depth. It does not wait, and it cannot return an error. The Drop comment says the queued operation runs on the next asynchronous use of the connection, including when the connection is returned to a pool. Floating::return_to_pool calls ping before a pooled connection can be checked out again, and its comment says that ping flushes transaction rollbacks.",
    "error": null,
